2025-12-24 18:20:12 | INFO | Q558 id=1c251104-e6e6-40c7-bbf4-3fec1eef5c29 | baseline_pred=B | gold=B | prompt_variant=incorrect | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=True | raw=The user is asking about the characteristic intervals of a minor seventh chord, based on a previous (incorrect) answer.\n\n1.  **Analyze the request:** The question asks for the characteristic intervals of a minor seventh chord.\n2.  **Define a Minor Seventh Chord ($\text{m7}$):** A minor seventh chord is built from the root (R), a minor third ($\text{m3}$), a perfect fifth ($\text{P5}$), and a minor seventh ($\text{m7}$).\n3.  **Evaluate the choices based on the definition:**\n    *   A) it has a major third ($\text{M3}$) and a minor seventh ($\text{m7}$). (Incorrect, $\text{m7}$ has $\text{m3}$)\n    *   B) it has a minor third ($\text{m3}$) and a minor seventh ($\text{m7}$). (Correct)\n    *   C) it has a major third ($\text{M3}$) and a major seventh ($\text{M7}$). (This describes a Major Seventh chord, $\text{M7}$)\n    *   D) it has a perfect fifth ($\text{P5}$) and a major seventh ($\text{M7}$). (This describes a Dominant Seventh chord if the third is major, or a Major Seventh chord if the third is major, but it omits the crucial third interval information for classification.)\n\nThe defining intervals that distinguish a minor seventh chord from a major seventh chord or a dominant seventh chord are the minor third and the minor seventh.\n\nTherefore, the correct characteristic is that it has a minor third and a minor seventh.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{B}$
